  local, and static-shared bytes, registers per thread, and the maximum threads
  per block for the exact loaded kernel. The same probe queries CUDA's maximum
  active blocks per multiprocessor for the actual 256-thread, zero-dynamic-
  shared launch and records the device maximum resident threads per SM.

  Live RTX 4060 development checks at N10/N14 report 188 constant bytes for the
  tritwise path and 59,237 for both lookup paths, exactly matching the generated
  source declarations; static-shared and local bytes are zero. Registers per
  thread range from 35 through 38 across those six loaded functions, with a
  1,024-thread function/device block ceiling. All six N10/N14 functions admit
  six active 256-thread blocks per SM, or 1,536 resident threads against the
  device maximum of 1,536.

  This is theoretical launch occupancy from CUDA's calculator, not observed SM
  utilization. These are correctness/resource observations, not semantic
  authority.

