  A deterministic `cuda-crazy-lookup-address-fanout-v1` diagnostic now applies
  the same five-trit table-address formula to the canonical full-domain crazy
  search order and its production exact 1,024-position projection. NVIDIA's
  Best Practices Guide 13.3 states that different constant-memory addresses
  requested within a warp are serialized with cost scaling by unique address
  count; the diagnostic therefore reports exact address fanout, not hit rate.
  For ordinary full-domain order, the low lookup has 32 unique addresses in
  1,845 of 1,846 warps (the final nine-lane warp has nine), while the middle
  lookup has one unique address in 1,611 warps and two in 235.

  For the exact 1,024-position projection, all 32 warps have 32 unique low
  addresses and one unique middle address. Projection therefore does not
  relieve the low-lookup
  serialization pressure, while it makes the middle lookup uniform. The exact
  per-warp counts are retained under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-09-04-crazy-lookup-address-fanout/`
  from clean model commit `bf6cfbc3`.

